{
 "provenance": {
  "recorded": "2026-08-12",
  "how": "Captured from a build that passed the 22-case cross-check against the Java implementation these vectors originally came from, at reference commit 726af429711759e41a32111b33e6c1327f025838. That harness has since been removed: the generator is pinned by these vectors now, and the project no longer depends on the original to build or test.",
  "added-2026-08-13": "The two 'float compare boundary' cases were added later and never saw that cross-check, so their expected values were established from the semantics instead of from our own output. p=0.8 and p=0.6 are exact multiples of 2^-24 (13421773 and 10066330), so nextFloat() can land exactly on them; p=0.4 and p=0.2 cannot. Java evaluates (double)nextFloat() < p and we evaluate f < p -- both comparisons are strict, so a draw landing exactly on p is a non-match either way, independent of the narrowing. Columns (269,4168) at y=-63 and (1533,851) at y=-62 are such draws for seed 12345 and must stay absent. Verified load-bearing by mutation: changing bd_probe's `<` to `<=` passes every other case here and fp_proof, and fails these two.",
  "warning": "A diff against these is a bug in this repo. Do not re-record to make a red gate green -- see the header of tools/check.py.",
  "added-2026-08-13-selectivity": "The 'selectivity sort permutes' case was recorded from the build immediately BEFORE main.c began sorting the pattern by selectivity, so it pins the pre-sort match set. The sort is a pure reordering -- bd_probe seeds only from (x+dx, y, z+dz) and carries no state between blocks, so bd_check is a conjunction of independent predicates -- and this vector is what holds that claim to it. It is not an independent check of the generator itself; the 23 cases above are."
 },
